00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:15.130
using Python, R, or any
other language or system.

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:21.055
Now many of you may be like
me, and when you started your data

00:03:21.055 --> 00:03:25.295
science journey, the idea
of working with APIs,

00:03:25.295 --> 00:03:29.840
let alone creating your own API
was so daunting that I honestly

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.960
tried to avoid working with
APIs at all cost. Well,

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:35.680
I'm here to tell you that
they're not that scary and can

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.145
actually be used to vastly
improve your data science workflows.

00:03:40.145 --> 00:03:43.425
Now the acronym API stands
for application programming

00:03:43.425 --> 00:03:47.025
interface, and they
essentially allow communication

00:03:47.025 --> 00:03:49.270
between applications.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:53.670
To help explain what APIs are,
I like to think of the commonly

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.965
used restaurant analogy you
see on your screen here.

00:03:56.965 --> 00:04:00.405
Here we have three customers
that have entered a restaurant,

00:04:00.405 --> 00:04:02.565
and would like to order some
food that the chef in the

00:04:02.565 --> 00:04:04.230
kitchen will prepare.

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.110
To request food,

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:09.190
the customers need some way to
communicate their order to the kitchen,

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:15.245
and rarely if ever the customer
will interact directly with the chef.

00:04:15.245 --> 00:04:18.685
So that's where the waiter
or waitress comes into play.

00:04:18.685 --> 00:04:20.960
They take the order
from the customer,

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:23.920
and can communicate
that order to the chef.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:25.440
The chef prepares the food,

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.035
which is then delivered
back to the customer.

00:04:28.035 --> 00:04:29.075
So in this analogy,

00:04:29.075 --> 00:04:32.595
the waiter or waitress
allows for communication between the

00:04:32.595 --> 00:04:36.215
customers and the chef,
essentially they are the API.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:39.610
For data scientists,

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:43.290
APIs can be an essential
workflow building block that

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:47.205
gives other users or
applications or systems,

00:04:47.205 --> 00:04:50.645
the ability to interact
with the functions you've designed,

00:04:50.645 --> 00:04:54.820
the models you've trained,
or the data you've curated.

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:57.700
You also don't need to
be a software engineer or computer

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:59.700
scientist to create an API,

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:02.740
and there are numerous
frameworks in both r and Python

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:06.555
for creating APIs,
including plumber and Flask.

00:05:06.555 --> 00:05:10.830
Now for today's workflow, we're
gonna focus on just a FastAPI,

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:13.630
which is one of the most
popular frameworks for creating

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:18.915
APIs using Python given
its simplicity and performance.

00:05:18.915 --> 00:05:21.715
Now as I previously
mentioned, the FastAPI

00:05:21.715 --> 00:05:25.235
we are gonna build today
and publish to Posit Connect will

00:05:25.235 --> 00:05:28.080
essentially serve as
a gateway to our data.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:31.600
Which can be accessed
by a variety of content.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.160
But like, why would you
want to do this? Well,

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:35.605
there are a few reasons.

00:05:35.605 --> 00:05:38.485
The first reason is that
it simplifies your code.

00:05:38.485 --> 00:05:42.085
You no longer need to attach
and read in and transform the

00:05:42.085 --> 00:05:46.850
data into each individual piece
of content. So this helps also

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:49.010
prevent data duplication

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:53.225
and also standardize data
access across content.

00:05:53.225 --> 00:05:57.065
Secondly, by putting data behind
an API, it makes it

00:05:57.065 --> 00:06:00.390
easily accessible to
different content types.

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:01.110
For example,

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:04.870
you can have a Jupyter Notebook
built using Python and a Shiny

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:10.345
application built using R access the
same data via the same API.

00:06:10.345 --> 00:06:14.425
Later on in this
demo, we'll demonstrate this capability.

00:06:14.425 --> 00:06:18.030
Lastly, by publishing the
FastAPI to Posit Connect,

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:21.070
you can control access
to your data by adding an

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:23.390
authentication
layer to your APIs.

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:26.030
And this is a great option
if your team is working with

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:28.575
sensitive data.

00:06:28.575 --> 00:06:31.535
Now we are very close
to building our FastAPI,

00:06:31.535 --> 00:06:35.375
but I just want to spend
one minute and explain the data

00:06:35.375 --> 00:06:38.800
we're going to have
behind this API.

