This data is
completely made up,

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.040
and I wanted to make it
as simple as possible given our

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:46.865
limited time today. Now this
data, it's also relatively small.

00:06:46.865 --> 00:06:51.105
It's only about five hundred
rows and has only two columns.

00:06:51.105 --> 00:06:55.110
So the first column is a
random letter in the English alphabet,

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:58.390
and the second column is
a random number from one to

00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:00.895
twenty, and that's
pretty much it.

00:07:00.895 --> 00:07:02.335
As we go through today's demo,

00:07:02.335 --> 00:07:06.335
I want you to think about
where your data lives today. In this

00:07:06.335 --> 00:07:10.560
workflow, our data is gonna
live as a local CSV file,

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:14.560
but maybe your data lives in
a database or a shared network

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:18.035
drive or maybe as a
pin on Posit Connect.

00:07:18.035 --> 00:07:22.355
By using an API to access
the data, no matter where it lives,

00:07:22.355 --> 00:07:25.955
we can standardize how the
data is accessed across your various

00:07:25.955 --> 00:07:31.450
pieces of content, and
your entire data science team.

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:34.250
Okay. So let's
build our FastAPI.

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:38.235
On this slide, is all of
the code needed to create a

00:07:38.235 --> 00:07:41.115
simple data gateway FastAPI.

00:07:41.115 --> 00:07:45.360
And as you can see, it's less
than twenty lines of code.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.160
In the first three lines
of code, we will import the

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:51.200
libraries, modules, and
functions needed for this

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:54.675
workflow, including
FastAPI itself,

00:07:54.675 --> 00:07:58.995
the typing module for hinting
at content types, and finally

00:07:58.995 --> 00:08:04.100
the Pandas library for reading
in and manipulating our data.

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:06.500
We next create the FastAPI

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:09.540
instance using the
FastAPI function,

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:13.455
which we'll call
app in this example.

00:08:13.455 --> 00:08:17.535
Now the first step of our API
is to read in the data as a

00:08:17.535 --> 00:08:23.140
dataframe using the read_csv
function from pandas.

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:26.820
We'll then store our dataframe
as a variable called large_df.

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:32.115
Next, we define
our API endpoint.

00:08:32.115 --> 00:08:37.315
Now an endpoint takes in the
API request, which is usually a URL,

00:08:37.315 --> 00:08:40.560
processes it and
sends back a response.

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:42.160
For our FastAPI,

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:46.000
we are using a Git method
for our endpoint, which is

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:48.635
routinely used to read data.

00:08:48.635 --> 00:08:53.195
The forward slash followed by
the word data tells the FastAPI

00:08:53.195 --> 00:08:56.315
that anything following the
forward slash data in the

00:08:56.315 --> 00:08:59.390
request URL are
parameters to the API,

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:04.350
which can be fed
into the function directly below.

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:06.595
And finally, we have
the function itself.

00:09:06.595 --> 00:09:10.515
This is where all of the
logic of your FastAPI lives.

00:09:10.515 --> 00:09:12.035
In our FastAPI

00:09:12.035 --> 00:09:15.362
we are defining a
single function called

00:09:15.362 --> 00:09:16.990
get_filtered_data,

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:20.750
and it takes a single
argument called letters.

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:25.535
Letter are captured as a list
of strings when the API is queried.

00:09:25.535 --> 00:09:29.775
It's also an optional query
item and defaults to none if

00:09:29.775 --> 00:09:32.500
no letters are provided.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:36.980
So we next use the list
of letters to filter our data.

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:40.740
And if I simply read this
code left to right top to

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:43.355
bottom, we start by asking

00:09:43.355 --> 00:09:47.435
if the query contained any
letters. If yes, the data is

00:09:47.435 --> 00:09:50.470
then filtered for
those specific letters,

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:53.270
and returned by the function.

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:57.750
If no letters are provided,
it simply returns the entire

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:00.775
dataset itself.

00:10:00.775 --> 00:10:03.095
So now that we have
all the code needed,

00:10:03.095 --> 00:10:06.055
I'm going to copy
