00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:31.900
And here we can add letters,

00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:36.525
so I'll add an "A" here,
and then we can hit execute. And

00:14:36.525 --> 00:14:39.645
once we hit execute,
the swagger interface,

00:14:39.645 --> 00:14:44.800
it'll show you the curl command
that was run to ping the API,

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:47.280
the request URL,
which is right here,

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:49.200
and then if we scroll down,

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:52.705
we can see the response
which is in this JSON format,

00:14:52.705 --> 00:14:56.785
where each row of the data
is a chunk of this JSON file.

00:14:56.785 --> 00:14:59.665
Let's try a few different
combinations just to make sure

00:14:59.665 --> 00:15:01.990
that the API is
behaving correctly.

00:15:01.990 --> 00:15:04.230
So let me come back
up here to the top,

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:08.390
and we'll add an additional
string item. So maybe we'll do

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:14.025
let's try "T", and we'll
also try "M" Hit execute,

00:15:14.025 --> 00:15:18.490
and we can see the curl
response, the request URL,

00:15:18.490 --> 00:15:24.170
and here we can see the
response versus just Ts and Ms.

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:28.705
Now, a major benefit of this
API hosted on Posit Connect is

00:15:28.705 --> 00:15:34.130
that you can manage who has access
to it, which is shown right over here.

00:15:34.130 --> 00:15:37.970
If I select any of
the options that require authentication

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:41.570
including this bottom option for
specific users or groups or

00:15:41.570 --> 00:15:45.015
the middle option all
users login required,

00:15:45.015 --> 00:15:48.695
then users of the API
would essentially need to provide an

00:15:48.695 --> 00:15:53.600
API key in order to leverage
this API. And that API key,

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:56.400
you can actually login to
connect, click on your name,

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:58.580
and generate those
keys right over here.

00:16:01.955 --> 00:16:06.370
So now that we have the
FastAPI hosted on Connect, let's use it

00:16:06.370 --> 00:16:12.850
as a gateway for multiple pieces
of content using both R and Python.

00:16:12.850 --> 00:16:15.925
And let's actually start with
Python in a Jupyter Notebook.

00:16:15.925 --> 00:16:19.685
So let's come back to Posit
Workbench, so we have our three

00:16:19.685 --> 00:16:21.605
sessions currently running,

00:16:21.605 --> 00:16:24.260
and I'm gonna open
up this Jupyter notebook session,

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:27.620
which I have open in
a separate tab here,

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:30.660
and here's the Jupyter
notebook that I created.

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:33.655
So in this notebook, in this
first code chunk here, we have

00:16:33.655 --> 00:16:38.215
few packages and libraries
we need for this analysis,

00:16:38.215 --> 00:16:40.935
specifically this request
library which is going to be

00:16:40.935 --> 00:16:44.680
important for pinging our API.
So let's run this code chunk.

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:49.780
In the next code
chunk right down here,

00:16:49.780 --> 00:16:52.645
we're gonna use that
request library.

00:16:52.645 --> 00:16:56.885
To submit a get query to
our API. So you can see that

00:16:56.885 --> 00:17:00.565
request URL right here, and
we're gonna feed in two parameters,

00:17:00.565 --> 00:17:03.580
so we're essentially gonna
extract data where the letters

00:17:03.580 --> 00:17:07.920
column is equal to f, g, or
r. So let's go ahead and run this.

00:17:09.955 --> 00:17:12.515
And we're gonna take
that JSON response,

00:17:12.515 --> 00:17:16.195
and we're gonna convert it
into a dataframe using the Pandas

00:17:16.195 --> 00:17:18.640
package. So we'll
run this code chunk,

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:21.600
and you can see
the output of that

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:24.065
the query for the API.

00:17:24.065 --> 00:17:25.185
And then finally,

00:17:25.185 --> 00:17:28.225
we're gonna take this resulting
data frame that we received

00:17:28.225 --> 00:17:31.980
from the API, and we're gonna
create a plot to visualize it.

00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:34.540
And we do that
using the swarmplot

00:17:34.540 --> 00:17:37.980
from the seaborne
package in Python.

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:40.035
So also it's just worth noting,

00:17:40.035 --> 00:17:43.795
that I didn't have to
manually upload and filter the data.

00:17:43.795 --> 00:17:47.620
You know, the fast
API does this for us.

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:52.020
So that's how to query the
API from within a Python Jupyter notebook.

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:55.675
Let's actually switch gears to the
